.\" mutos_c0.1 - manual page for the MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler front end
.\" Formatted with the standard man(7) macro set (.TH/.SH/.TP/...).
.\" View with: man ./mutos_c0.1   or   groff -man -Tascii mutos_c0.1 | less
.TH MUTOS_C0 1 "September 2026" "MUTOS 1700" "MUTOS 1700 Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
mutos_c0 \- MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler, first pass (front end)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutos_c0
.I source
.I temp1
.I temp2
.RB [ \-P ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_c0
is the first of the two passes that make up the MUTOS 1700 cross C
compiler (the second is
.BR mutos_c1 (1)).
It lexes and parses
.IR source ,
which is normally the already macro-expanded output of
.BR mutos_cpp (1)
rather than raw C source, and writes a tagged intermediate-code
byte stream to
.I temp1
(with any string-literal text routed through
.I temp2
instead \(en see
.BR OUTPUT ).
.PP
This is a from-scratch, modern-C11 reimplementation of the observable
behavior of the original MUTOS 1700 / Version 7 Unix
.BR cc (1)
compiler's first pass, not a literal port of that pointer-arithmetic
K&R source; see
.I src/mutos_cc/README.md
in the toolchain source tree for the confirmed
.IR temp1 / temp2
wire format (reverse-engineered byte-for-byte from real MUTOS 1700
hardware output) and the exact grammar this pass currently accepts.
.SH "CURRENT SCOPE"
.B mutos_c0
does not yet implement the full K&R C grammar. As of this writing it
accepts only:
.PP
.RS
.nf
translation-unit  := extdef*
extdef            := IDENT '(' ')' compound-stmt
compound-stmt     := '{' decl* stmt* '}'
decl              := 'int' IDENT (',' IDENT)* ';'
stmt              := assign-stmt | return-stmt
assign-stmt       := IDENT '=' expr ';'
return-stmt       := 'return' expr? ';'
expr              := integer arithmetic ('+ \- * / % ~', unary \-/+,
                      parens, variable references)
.fi
.RE
.PP
i.e. a function definition takes no parameters. Its body may declare
any number of plain
.B int
locals (no initializers) before its statements, matching K&R's
declarations-before-statements block structure; each statement is
either a single-variable assignment or a
.BR return .
An expression stays an unmaterialized compile-time constant for as
long as every operand feeding it is also constant \(en matching the
real compiler's own per-operation front-end constant folding \(en and
is emitted as a real variable-reference/operator tree the moment a
variable is involved. Every function is
implicitly
.BR int -returning,
matching K&R default-int semantics.
.PP
Anything outside this scope (a second kind of statement, a
non-.BR int
declaration, a compound/relational/logical/shift/assignment operator,
a
non-constant expression, function parameters, control flow, and so
on) is rejected with a clear diagnostic and a non-zero exit status,
never silently miscompiled. See
.I src/mutos_cc/README.md
for the current expansion plan.
.SH OUTPUT
.I temp1
receives the bulk of the intermediate code: a sequence of tagged
items, each either a single-byte operator code (immediately followed
by the fixed sentinel byte 0xFE), a little-endian 16-bit data word, or
a NUL-terminated symbol name.
.I temp2
receives only string-literal text (not used by any construct in the
current grammar scope, so it is presently always empty apart from its
own terminating marker). This exactly mirrors the real MUTOS 1700
compiler's own
.IR /lib/c0 ,
which
.BR mutos_cc (1)
(once written \(en see
.BR BUGS )
will invoke in the same way.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I source
Path to the input file (normally
.BR mutos_cpp (1) 's
output). Read directly if given a raw
.B .c
file instead \(en the lexer tolerates comments and whitespace itself
\(en which is convenient for manual testing but not how the real
pipeline invokes this pass.
.TP
.I temp1
Output path for the primary intermediate-code stream.
.TP
.I temp2
Output path for the string-literal side stream.
.TP
.B \-P
Accepted for command-line compatibility with the real MUTOS 1700
.IR /lib/c0 's
profiling-flag position (see
.IR v7/cc/cc.c ).
Not yet acted upon: the current grammar scope has no function bodies
where profiling instrumentation would matter.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors are printed to standard error as
.RI \(dq file : line ": error: " message \(dq
and cause a non-zero exit status; processing continues to the end of
input so that further problems in the same source can be reported in
one pass, matching
.BR mutos_cpp (1) 's
diagnostic convention.
.SH BUGS
.B mutos_cc,
the driver that would normally chain
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
.BR mutos_c0 (1),
.BR mutos_c1 (1),
.BR mutos_as (1),
and
.BR mutos_ld (1)
together into a single pipeline, does not exist yet; invoke this pass
directly (see
.BR SYNOPSIS ),
or see
.I tests/mutos_cc/run_goldens.sh
for a scripted example.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mutos_c1 (1),
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
.BR mutos_as (1)
